{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_status","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

Prefer describe-environment: it also lists services that exist only in the staged patch, unattached volumes, and each resource's staged state. Get the deployment status of a Railway project environment. Returns project + environment metadata, whether the environment has undeployed staged changes, the environment's buckets, and for each live service its latest deployment status, replica count, cron schedule, and attached volumes. Also includes `pendingWork`: the environment's pending (staged/applying) work that is not live yet — a staged patch or a commit still rolling out (poll until the operation leaves this list). If environmentId is omitted, the project's `production` environment is used when present, otherwise the oldest environment. This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_status(args: {
  // Environment to report on. Defaults to `production` if present, otherwise the project's oldest environment.
  environmentId?: string;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ buckets: Array<{
  // Bucket ID
  id: string;
  name: string;
  region: string | null;
}>; environment: {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  // Changes staged in this environment but not deployed yet, or null when nothing is staged. Commit them with accept-deploy, or use get-service-config to see them per service.
  stagedChanges: {
  // Number of pending change entries in the staged patch across services, volumes, buckets and shared variables (the same entries listed under pendingWork)
  changeCount: number;
  patchId: string;
  // STAGED, or APPLYING while a commit is in flight
  patchStatus: string;
} | null;
  // Changes not yet merged from a forked environment — unrelated to staged changes
  unmergedChangesCount: number | null;
}; pendingWork: Array<{
  // Summary of the changes carried by this operation
  changes: Array<unknown>;
  // Operation id (`patch:<patchId>` or `deploy:<deploymentId>`), pollable via environmentPendingWork
  id: string;
  // Source of the operation (e.g. EnvironmentPatch, Deployment)
  kind: string;
  // When the operation started (ISO 8601)
  startedAt: string;
  // staged = written but not committed; applying = commit in flight, not live yet
  status: "staged" | "applying";
  // Temporal workflow driving the operation, if any
  workflowId: string | null;
}>; project: { id: string; name: string; workspaceName: string | null; }; services: Array<{ cronSchedule: string | null; id: string; latestDeployment: { createdAt: string; id: string; status: string; url: string | null; } | null; name: string; numReplicas: number | null; volumes: Array<{
  // Volume ID
  id: string;
  mountPath: string;
  name: string;
  region: string | null;
  sizeMB: number;
}>; }>; }>>; };
```"}

{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_list_deployments","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

List recent deployments for a Railway project, optionally filtered by environment, service, or status. Returns the most recent first, with who triggered each one, its regions, and whether it can be redeployed or rolled back. This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_list_deployments(args: {
  // Filter to a specific environment
  environmentId?: string;
  // Max deployments to return (default 10, max 50)
  limit?: number;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
  // Filter to a specific service
  serviceId?: string;
  // Filter to deployments with this status (e.g. SUCCESS, FAILED, CRASHED, BUILDING, DEPLOYING)
  status?: "WAITING" | "NEEDS_APPROVAL" | "QUEUED" | "INITIALIZING" | "SKIPPED" | "BUILDING" | "DEPLOYING" | "SUCCESS" | "FAILED" | "REMOVED" | "CRASHED" | "REMOVING" | "SLEEPING";
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ deployments: Array<{
  // Whether this deployment's build can be re-run
  canRedeploy: boolean;
  // Whether this deployment can be made live again
  canRollback: boolean;
  createdAt: string;
  // User who triggered the deployment, if one did
  creator: { email: string; id: string; name: string | null; } | null;
  environmentId: string;
  id: string;
  meta: { branch?: string; commitHash?: string; commitMessage?: string; image?: string; reason?: string; } | null;
  // Regions the deployment's instances were placed in
  regions: Array<string>;
  serviceId: string | null;
  snapshotId: string | null;
  status: string;
  updatedAt: string;
  url: string | null;
}>; }>>; };
```"}

{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_describe_service","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

Everything about one service in an environment: its config with staged changes applied (source, build, deploy, networking, volume mounts), the per-field staged changes pending on it, mounted volumes, live domains and TCP proxies, the latest deployment, and its tracing state. `state` says whether the service is live, staged-create, staged-delete, or live-with-staged-changes. Variable names are listed but values are not — use list-variables for values. If environmentId is omitted, the production environment is used. This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_describe_service(args: {
  // Environment ID. Defaults to `production` if present, otherwise the project's oldest environment.
  environmentId?: string;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
  // The service ID
  serviceId: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // The service's config in this environment with staged changes applied: source, build, deploy, networking, volume mounts. Variable values and registry credentials are omitted.
  config: { [key: string]: unknown; };
  // Live domains; null when the service is not live yet or the lookup failed
  domains: { customDomains: Array<{ domain: string; id: string; targetPort: number | null; }>; serviceDomains: Array<{ domain: string; id: string; targetPort: number | null; }>; } | null;
  environment: { id: string; name: string; };
  latestDeployment: { createdAt: string; id: string; status: string; url: string | null; } | null;
  service: {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  // live: exists and has nothing staged. staged-create: only exists in the staged patch, not provisioned until accept-deploy. staged-delete: live now, removed when the patch is committed. live-with-staged-changes: live with pending config changes.
  state: "live" | "staged-create" | "staged-delete" | "live-with-staged-changes";
};
  // The environment's pending patch, or null when nothing is staged
  staged: {
  changeCount: number;
  createdAt: string;
  message: string | null;
  patchId: string;
  // STAGED: pending, commit with accept-deploy. APPLYING: a commit is in progress; wait for it to settle before staging more.
  status: "STAGED" | "APPLYING";
  updatedAt: string;
} | null;
  // Pending changes to this service, per field
  stagedChanges: Array<{
  destructive: boolean;
  displayName: string;
  // Live value as displayed; empty when added
  from: string;
  // Config path, e.g. services.<id>.deploy.startCommand
  path: string;
  // Staged value as displayed; empty when removed
  to: string;
  type: "added" | "removed" | "updated";
}>;
  // Live TCP proxies; null when the service is not live yet or the lookup failed
  tcpProxies: Array<{
  applicationPort: number;
  domain: string;
  // Public endpoint to connect to, host:port
  endpoint: string;
  id: string;
  proxyPort: number;
  syncStatus: string;
}> | null;
  // The service's tracing state in this environment. Change it with update-service (tracing) or set-service-tracing.
  tracing: {
  // Whether auto-instrumentation is in effect. The switch only does anything while tracing is enabled.
  autoInstrumentationActive: boolean;
  // The service's auto-instrumentation switch: Railway instruments its processes with eBPF (OBI) for supported runtimes, no code changes
  autoInstrumentationEnabled: boolean;
  // Whether requests to the service are traced in this environment: the edge records a span per client-facing request and the next deploy provides the OpenTelemetry exporter variables
  tracingEnabled: boolean;
};
  variableNames: Array<string>;
  volumes: Array<{
  id: string;
  mountPath: string | null;
  name: string;
  region: string | null;
  sizeMB: number | null;
  // live: exists and has nothing staged. staged-create: only exists in the staged patch, not provisioned until accept-deploy. staged-delete: live now, removed when the patch is committed. live-with-staged-changes: live with pending config changes.
  state: "live" | "staged-create" | "staged-delete" | "live-with-staged-changes";
}>;
}>>; };
```"}

{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_staged_changes","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

Show the changes staged in a Railway environment that accept-deploy would commit: per resource (service, volume, bucket, group) the action — create, delete, update — and each changed field with its live and staged value. Variable values and registry credentials are redacted to names. Also reports whether the patch is destructive and whether a commit is already applying. Review this before calling accept-deploy. If environmentId is omitted, the production environment is used. This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_staged_changes(args: {
  // Environment to inspect. Defaults to `production` if present, otherwise the project's oldest environment.
  environmentId?: string;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // True when committing would delete a service, volume or bucket, or otherwise destroy data. accept-deploy may refuse such commits; the user can apply them from the dashboard.
  destructive: boolean;
  environment: { id: string; name: string; };
  // Staged private-networking toggle, if any
  privateNetworkDisabled: {
  destructive: boolean;
  displayName: string;
  // Live value as displayed; empty when added
  from: string;
  // Config path, e.g. services.<id>.deploy.startCommand
  path: string;
  // Staged value as displayed; empty when removed
  to: string;
  type: "added" | "removed" | "updated";
} | null;
  project: { id: string; name: string; };
  // Staged changes grouped by the service, volume, bucket or group they touch
  resources: Array<{ action: "create" | "delete" | "update"; changes: Array<{
  destructive: boolean;
  displayName: string;
  // Live value as displayed; empty when added
  from: string;
  // Config path, e.g. services.<id>.deploy.startCommand
  path: string;
  // Staged value as displayed; empty when removed
  to: string;
  type: "added" | "removed" | "updated";
}>; id: string; kind: "service" | "volume" | "bucket" | "group"; name: string; }>;
  // Staged shared-variable changes, names only
  sharedVariables: Array<{
  destructive: boolean;
  displayName: string;
  // Live value as displayed; empty when added
  from: string;
  // Config path, e.g. services.<id>.deploy.startCommand
  path: string;
  // Staged value as displayed; empty when removed
  to: string;
  type: "added" | "removed" | "updated";
}>;
  // The environment's pending patch, or null when nothing is staged
  staged: {
  changeCount: number;
  createdAt: string;
  message: string | null;
  patchId: string;
  // STAGED: pending, commit with accept-deploy. APPLYING: a commit is in progress; wait for it to settle before staging more.
  status: "STAGED" | "APPLYING";
  updatedAt: string;
} | null;
}>>; };
```"}

{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_logs","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

Get logs from Railway — deploy (runtime), build, http (proxy request), network-flow (per-connection egress/ingress) and dns (name lookups) streams. Pass a deploymentId to read one deployment, or serviceId (+ optional environmentId) to read the service's logs across its recent deployments in that environment. Use `types` to choose which streams to return (default: ['deploy']). This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_get_logs(args: {
  // A specific deployment ID. Omit it and pass serviceId to read the service's logs across its recent deployments.
  deploymentId?: string;
  // ISO 8601 timestamp with an explicit timezone, at minute precision or finer. Example: "2026-07-27T15:30:00.000Z". Fractional seconds are optional; defaults to now.
  endDate?: string;
  // Environment ID to read logs from. Optional — when omitted with a serviceId, the project's production environment is used (or its oldest, if there is no production).
  environmentId?: string;
  // Optional log filter expression (Loki-style, same syntax as the Railway dashboard). Applies to build and deploy streams; http, network-flow and dns take their own @field filters (e.g. @status:500, @direction:egress, @rcode:NXDOMAIN).
  filter?: string;
  // Max log entries per stream (default 100, max 500)
  limit?: number;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
  // Service ID. With no deploymentId, returns the service's logs across its deployments in the environment (build logs come from the latest deployment).
  serviceId?: string;
  // ISO 8601 timestamp with an explicit timezone, at minute precision or finer. Example: "2026-07-27T14:30:00.000Z". Fractional seconds are optional.
  startDate?: string;
  // Array of log stream names. Example: ["build", "deploy"] or ["http", "network-flow", "dns"]. Omit to fetch only deploy logs.
  types?: Array<"build" | "deploy" | "http" | "network-flow" | "dns">;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  build?: Array<{ attributes: Array<{ key: string; value: string; }>; message: string; severity?: string; timestamp: string; }>;
  deploy?: Array<{ attributes: Array<{ key: string; value: string; }>; message: string; severity?: string; timestamp: string; }>;
  // The deployment read (deployment scope), or the service's latest deployment (service scope)
  deploymentId: string | null;
  dns?: Array<{ answers: Array<string>; cnameChain: Array<string>; deploymentId: string; qname: string; qtype: string; rcode: string; timestamp: string; zone: string; }>;
  environmentId: string;
  http?: Array<{ clientUa: string; host: string; httpStatus: number; method: string; path: string; srcIp: string; timestamp: string; totalDuration: number; upstreamErrors: string; }>;
  networkFlow?: Array<{ byteCount: number; captureEnd: string; captureStart: string; deploymentId: string; direction: string; dropCause: string | null; dstAddr: string; dstPort: number; flowState: string; latencyMs: number; packetCount: number; peerKind: string; peerServiceId: string | null; protocol: string; srcAddr: string; srcPort: number; }>;
  // deployment: one deployment's logs. service: the service's logs across its deployments in the environment
  scope: "deployment" | "service";
  serviceId: string | null;
  status: string | null;
}>>; };
```"}

{"name":"mcp__codex_apps__railway_list_variables","description":"Build and deploy apps

MCP server instructions: Use this MCP to deploy, manage, and debug applications on Railway. Call the direct tools for reads and routine operations — listing projects/services/variables/domains, inspecting config, status, logs, metrics, setting variables, updating service settings, creating services, and searching the Railway docs. Fall back to the local Railway CLI when needed. Confirm with the user before destructive actions (e.g. `accept-deploy`).

List all environment variables for a service, fully rendered (reference variables like ${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}} are resolved). With a Railway session or API token, values are returned in plaintext and may contain secrets. Connected OAuth apps receive variable names only. Sealed variables are always listed by name with no value — they are set, but nobody can read them back, so never recreate one. If environmentId is omitted, the production environment is used. This tool is part of plugin `Railway`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__railway_list_variables(args: {
  // Environment ID. Defaults to `production` if present, otherwise the project's oldest environment.
  environmentId?: string;
  // The project ID
  projectId: string;
  // The service ID
  serviceId: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  environment: { id: string; name: string; };
  // Names of the sealed variables, sorted. These are already set on the service; their values cannot be read back by anyone, including the user. Do not treat them as missing and do not recreate them.
  sealedVariableNames: Array<string>;
  service: { id: string; name: string; };
  // True when values were withheld and only names are returned
  valuesRedacted: boolean;
  // All variable names, sorted, including sealed ones
  variableNames: Array<string>;
  // Readable variables as a name → value map. Never includes sealed variables. Omitted for OAuth-authenticated clients, which receive names only.
  variables?: { [key: string]: string; };
}>>; };
```"}

